{"black_box_warning":{"current_value":"Boxed warning text is populated, but the source field cites DailyMed and the warning value redundantly includes both full-prescribing-information and highlights wording.","fda_source_urls":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/202067s008%2C203993s010lbl.pdf","https://api.fda.gov/drug/label.json?search=openfda.spl_set_id%3A%22de03bd69-2dca-459c-93b4-541fd3e9571c%22&limit=5","https://www.fda.gov/drugs/drug-safety-and-availability/fda-requiring-boxed-warning-updated-improve-safe-use-benzodiazepine-drug-class"],"proposed_value":"Retain the FDA boxed-warning content for ONFI/clobazam, but source it only to FDA accessdata/openFDA labeling; optional cleanup is to de-duplicate the warning to one FDA boxed-warning section.","rationale":"The FDA ONFI label verifies a boxed warning for concomitant opioid use, abuse/misuse/addiction, and dependence/withdrawal. DailyMed is not permissible for this audit field, so the source metadata must be replaced with FDA-only metadata.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified"},"blocking_questions":[],"fact_checks":[{"current_value":"clobazam","field":"generic_name","proposed_value":"clobazam","rationale":"FDA labeling, eMC SmPCs, NCBI LiverTox, and the Ng2011 RCT all identify the same active drug as clobazam.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/202067s008%2C203993s010lbl.pdf","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548865/"]},{"current_value":"","field":"alternate_generic_names","proposed_value":"","rationale":"No separate generic alias was identified in the checked trusted sources; N-desmethylclobazam is an active metabolite, not an alternate generic name for clobazam.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/202067s008%2C203993s010lbl.pdf","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548865/"]},{"current_value":"Frisium; Onfi; Perizam; Sympazan; Tapclob; Zacco","field":"trade_names","proposed_value":"Frisium; Onfi; Perizam; Sympazan; Tapclob; Urbanol; Zacco","rationale":"All listed trade names were supported by FDA/eMC/NCBI sources. NCBI LiverTox also lists Urbanol as a clobazam trade name. The deterministic suggestion to add 'Clobazam' as a trade name should not be applied because clobazam is the generic name, not a trade name.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"missing","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/202067s008%2C203993s010lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/210833s006lbl.pdf","https://www.medicines.org.uk/emc/product/100006/smpc","https://www.medicines.org.uk/emc/product/6913/smpc","https://www.medicines.org.uk/emc/product/8950/smpc","https://www.medicines.org.uk/emc/product/14934/smpc","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548865/"]},{"current_value":"Yes","field":"available_in_us","proposed_value":"Yes","rationale":"ONFI and SYMPAZAN are FDA-labeled clobazam products, supporting US availability.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/202067s008%2C203993s010lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/210833s006lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"2011","field":"year_fda_cleared","proposed_value":"2011","rationale":"The FDA ONFI and SYMPAZAN labels state initial US approval as 2011, so the deterministic concordance warning for this field is not supported.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/202067s008%2C203993s010lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/210833s006lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Tablet; oral suspension; oral film","field":"formulations_available","proposed_value":"Tablet; oral suspension; oral film","rationale":"ONFI labeling verifies tablet and oral suspension; SYMPAZAN labeling verifies oral film.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/202067s008%2C203993s010lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/210833s006lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"40 mg/day","field":"maximum_approved_daily_dose","proposed_value":"40 mg/day","rationale":"FDA ONFI dosing reaches 40 mg/day for patients over 30 kg; 40 mg/day is the maximum FDA-labeled daily dose.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/202067s008%2C203993s010lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"5 mg/day for patients <=30 kg; 10 mg/day for patients >30 kg","field":"minimum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"5 mg/day for patients <=30 kg; 10 mg/day for patients >30 kg","rationale":"FDA labeling gives these as starting doses and states the doses in the table have been shown effective, with increasing effect at higher doses.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/202067s008%2C203993s010lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"LGS: 5-40 mg/day divided BID depending on weight","field":"typical_doses_per_day","proposed_value":"LGS: 5-40 mg/day by weight; doses above 5 mg/day administered BID","rationale":"FDA labeling says a 5 mg/day dose can be given once daily, while daily doses greater than 5 mg should be divided twice daily.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/202067s008%2C203993s010lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Clobazam 36-42 h; active metabolite 71-82 h","field":"half_life_range","proposed_value":"Clobazam 36-42 h; active metabolite 71-82 h","rationale":"FDA labeling reports mean elimination half-lives of 36-42 hours for clobazam and 71-82 hours for N-desmethylclobazam.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/202067s008%2C203993s010lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Liver","field":"major_organ_for_metabolism","proposed_value":"Liver","rationale":"FDA labeling states clobazam is extensively metabolized in the liver.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/202067s008%2C203993s010lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Not a major inducer; substrate of CYP3A4/CYP2C19; weak CYP2D6 inhibition reported","field":"enzyme_inducing_or_inhibiting","proposed_value":"Weak CYP3A4 inducer; CYP2D6 inhibitor; clobazam is metabolized primarily by CYP3A4 and to a lesser extent by CYP2C19/CYP2B6; N-desmethylclobazam is mainly metabolized by CYP2C19","rationale":"FDA labeling supports CYP3A4/CYP2C19 metabolism and CYP2D6 inhibition, but the current text omits FDA-labeled weak CYP3A4 induction and CYP2B6 contribution.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/202067s008%2C203993s010lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"LGS; Adjunctive nonspecific epilepsy","field":"epilepsy_type","proposed_value":"LGS; Adjunctive nonspecific epilepsy","rationale":"FDA labeling supports adjunctive treatment of seizures associated with LGS; eMC SmPCs support adjunctive use in epilepsy outside the US.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/202067s008%2C203993s010lbl.pdf","https://www.medicines.org.uk/emc/product/8950/smpc","https://www.medicines.org.uk/emc/product/6913/smpc"]},{"current_value":"Exact mechanism is not fully understood; 1,5-benzodiazepine positive allosteric modulation of GABA-A receptors potentiates GABAergic neurotransmission.","field":"mechanism_of_action","proposed_value":"Exact mechanism is not fully understood; 1,5-benzodiazepine positive allosteric modulation of GABA-A receptors potentiates GABAergic neurotransmission.","rationale":"FDA labeling states that clobazam is a 1,5-benzodiazepine and its mechanism is thought to involve potentiation of GABAergic neurotransmission through the benzodiazepine site of the GABA-A receptor.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/202067s008%2C203993s010lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"FDA/DailyMed labeling","field":"mechanism_source","proposed_value":"FDA accessdata labeling","rationale":"The mechanism is supported by FDA labeling; DailyMed should be removed from the named source because the audit trusted-source list does not include DailyMed.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/202067s008%2C203993s010lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"High","field":"mechanism_confidence","proposed_value":"High","rationale":"Although the exact mechanism is not fully understood, the benzodiazepine/GABA-A mechanism is explicitly supported by FDA labeling.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/202067s008%2C203993s010lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"No clinically meaningful QT effect established","field":"qt_interval_effect","proposed_value":"No clinically meaningful QT effect established","rationale":"FDA labeling reports a thorough QT study in which ONFI did not prolong QTc to a clinically relevant extent at two times the maximum recommended dose.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/202067s008%2C203993s010lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"CNS: somnolence/sedation 26-32%, infectious: pyrexia 10%, GI: constipation 10%, psychiatric: aggression 8%, respiratory: cough 7%, neurologic: drooling 7%","field":"adverse_symptoms_percentages","proposed_value":"CNS: somnolence/sedation 26% overall (32% high dose); GI: constipation 5% overall (10% high dose); general: pyrexia 13% overall (12% high dose); infectious: upper respiratory tract infection 12% overall (14% high dose); neurologic: drooling 9% overall (14% high dose); psychiatric: aggression 8% overall (14% high dose); respiratory: cough 5% overall (7% high dose)","rationale":"The FDA adverse-reaction table supports somnolence/sedation 26% overall/32% high dose, constipation 5% overall/10% high dose, pyrexia 13% overall/12% high dose, upper respiratory tract infection 12% overall/14% high dose, drooling 9% overall/14% high dose, aggression 8% overall/14% high dose, and cough 5% overall/7% high dose. The current row mixes dose-specific and all-ONFI rates and misclassifies pyrexia as infectious.","requires_user_approval":true,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/202067s008%2C203993s010lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"CNS; GI; Infectious; Neurologic; Psychiatric; Respiratory","field":"filter_symptom_category","proposed_value":"CNS; GI; General; Infectious; Neurologic; Psychiatric; Respiratory","rationale":"After correcting the adverse-symptom text, pyrexia belongs under a general/systemic category while upper respiratory tract infection supports retaining Infectious.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"missing","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/202067s008%2C203993s010lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"FDA/DailyMed SPL; status=boxed_warning_found; setid=de03bd69-2dca-459c-93b4-541fd3e9571c; published=Dec 17, 2025; title=ONFI (CLOBAZAM) TABLET ONFI (CLOBAZAM) SUSPENSION [LUNDBECK PHARMACEUTICALS LLC]; url=https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=de03bd69-2dca-459c-93b4-541fd3e9571c","field":"fda_black_box_warning_source","proposed_value":"FDA accessdata drug label; status=boxed_warning_found; p